	doi = {10.1088/1748-9326/3/4/045011},
	abstract = {Observations from the moderate resolution imaging spectroradiometer ({MODIS}) were used in combination with a large data set of field measurements to map woody above-ground biomass ({AGB}) across tropical Africa. We generated a best-quality cloud-free mosaic of {MODIS} satellite reflectance observations for the period 2000–2003 and used a regression tree model to predict {AGB} at 1 km resolution. Results based on a cross-validation approach show that the model explained 82\% of the variance in {AGB}, with a root mean square error of 50.5 Mg ha−1 for a range of biomass between 0 and 454 Mg ha−1. Analysis of lidar metrics from the Geoscience Laser Altimetry System ({GLAS}), which are sensitive to vegetation structure, indicate that the model successfully captured the regional distribution of {AGB}. The results showed a strong positive correlation (R2 = 0.90) between the {GLAS} height metrics and predicted {AGB}.},
